      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L14-ACTIVITY-017",
      "SourceSection": "14.3: Solving Exponential Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "What is the relationship between these two solutions? Explain how you know.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L14-ACTIVITY-018",
      "SourceSection": "14.3: Solving Exponential Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "So far we have solved exponential equations by",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L14-ACTIVITY-019",
      "SourceSection": "14.3: Solving Exponential Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "finding whole number powers of the base (for example, the solution of \\(10^x = 100,\\!000\\) is 5)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L14-ACTIVITY-020",
      "SourceSection": "14.3: Solving Exponential Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "estimation (for example, the solution of \\(10^x = 300\\) is between 2 and 3)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L14-ACTIVITY-021",
      "SourceSection": "14.3: Solving Exponential Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "using a logarithm and approximating its value on a calculator (for example, the solution of \\(10^x = 300\\) is \\(\\log 300 \\approx 2.48\\))",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L14-ACTIVITY-022",
      "SourceSection": "14.3: Solving Exponential Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Sometimes solving exponential equations takes additional reasoning. Here are a couple of examples.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L14-ACTIVITY-023",
      "SourceSection": "14.3: Solving Exponential Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle \\begin {align} 5 \\boldcdot 10^x &= 45\\\\ 5 \\boldcdot 10^x &= 45\\\\10^x &= 9\\\\x &=\\log 9\\\\ \\end {align}\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L14-ACTIVITY-024",
      "SourceSection": "14.3: Solving Exponential Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle \\begin {align} 10^{(0.2t)} &= 1,\\!000\\\\ 10^{(0.2t)} &= 10^3\\\\ 0.2t &= 3\\\\ t &= \\frac {3}{0.2}\\\\ t &=15\\\\ \\end {align}\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L14-ACTIVITY-025",
      "SourceSection": "14.3: Solving Exponential Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "In the first example, the power of 10 is multiplied by 5, so to find the value of \\(x\\) that makes this equation true each side was divided by 5. From there, the equation was rewritten as a logarithm, giving an exact value for \\(x\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L14-ACTIVITY-026",
      "SourceSection": "14.3: Solving Exponential Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "In the second example, the expressions on each side of the equation were rewritten as powers of 10: \\(10^{(0.2t)}=10^3\\). This means that the exponent \\(0.2t\\) on one side and the 3 on the other side must be equal, and leads to a simpler…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L14-ACTIVITY-027",
      "SourceSection": "14.3: Solving Exponential Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "How do we solve an exponential equation with base \\(e\\), such as \\(e^x = 5\\)? We can express the solution using the natural logarithm, the logarithm for base \\(e\\). Natural logarithm is written as \\(\\ln\\), or sometimes as \\(\\log_e\\). Just…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L14-ACTIVITY-028",
      "SourceSection": "14.3: Solving Exponential Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "All this means that we can solve \\(e^x = 5\\) by rewriting the equation as \\(x = \\ln 5\\). This says that \\(x\\) is the exponent to which base \\(e\\) is raised to equal 5.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L14-ACTIVITY-029",
      "SourceSection": "14.3: Solving Exponential Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "To estimate the size of \\(\\ln 5\\), remember that \\(e\\) is about 2.7. Because 5 is greater than \\(e^1\\), this means that \\(\\ln 5\\) is greater than 1. \\(e^2\\) is about \\((2.7)^2\\) or 7.3. Because 5 is less than \\(e^2\\), this means that \\(\\ln…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L14-ACTIVITY-030",
      "SourceSection": "14.3: Solving Exponential Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The number \\(e\\) is an irrational number with an infinite decimal expansion that starts \\(2.71828182845\\ .\\ .\\ .\\), which is used in finance and science as the base for an exponential function.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L14-ACTIVITY-031",
      "SourceSection": "14.3: Solving Exponential Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "$e$ (mathematical constant)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L14-ACTIVITY-032",
      "SourceSection": "14.3: Solving Exponential Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The natural logarithm of \\(x\\), written \\(\\ln(x)\\), is the log to the base \\(e\\) of \\(x\\). So it is the number \\(y\\) that makes the equation \\(e^y = x\\) true.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
